    "ratioDecidendi": "Serious misconduct in an employment contract is judged by the common law principles on repudiatory breach in RDC Concrete, so a breach of a term that the parties intended as a condition can be serious misconduct whatever its consequences; here the duty to serve the company faithfully was such a condition. The breaches were not 'wilful' because the employee genuinely believed his practice was permitted, but the serious misconduct finding allowed the employer to terminate without notice, and the employer could rely on misconduct discovered after its termination letter by analogy with Boston Deep Sea Fishing. Section 108 of the Evidence Act is an exception that applies only where the party first establishes a prima facie case, which the employer had not done for the counterclaimed expenses.",
