    "court": "SGHC",
    "year": 2013,
    "subjectArea": "Employment Law / Constructive Dismissal and Damages; Civil Procedure / Striking Out and Expunging Affidavit Evidence",
    "facts": "An employee tendered his resignation and then claimed constructive dismissal, alleging the employer persecuted him because he is a homosexual. His contract allowed termination by two months' notice or two months' pay in lieu, without reasons, and the employer had paid him four months' salary; the employer applied to strike out the claim.",
    "issues": "(1) Whether affidavit paragraphs that reproduced the contents of documents the employee was barred from exhibiting should be expunged; (2) whether damages for constructive dismissal can exceed the contractual payment for a lawful termination; (3) whether a bare plea of breach of the implied term of trust and confidence is enough to claim further damages.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "Where the contract allows termination on notice or pay in lieu without reasons, an employee who claims constructive dismissal is confined to the sum payable for a lawful termination unless he proves a breach of the implied term of trust and confidence, pleaded and particularised, with resulting loss. The bare fact of termination cannot found damages beyond the contractual entitlement, because otherwise every employee could avoid a termination clause by alleging a breach of trust and confidence.",
    "significance": "Confirms that a constructive dismissal claim does not automatically exceed the contractual notice or pay-in-lieu entitlement, and that a breach of trust and confidence must be properly pleaded to go further.",
    "statutesCited": [],
    "unreadable": false,
    "notes": "Read in full. Decided 26 December 2013 (heard 9 September 2013). The neutral citation [2013] SGHC 279 is printed on the judgment; the file name gives [2014] 1 SLR 1382. An editorial note says the plaintiff's appeal (Civil Appeal No 126 of 2013) was scheduled for the Court of Appeal in May 2014; the file does not show the outcome, so this decision may have been varied. The plaintiff appeared in person before the judge. The judgment cites no statute."
