    "facts": "Bluestone Corp Pte Ltd, a medical supplies company owned 65% by Henry and 35% by its director Phang, sued Phang, his Malaysian company Bluestone Healthcare Sdn Bhd (incorporated in 2004), and employee Hooi Loo, who was later a director of that company, for breach of fiduciary duties, knowing assistance, passing off and conspiracy. Phang and Hooi Loo were dismissed in August 2018, and the court found that the two shareholder-directors had informally agreed in the company's early days that each could pursue medical-sector business in Malaysia independently.",
    "issues": "Whether Phang breached his fiduciary duties in setting up and running Healthcare, Prius and Primuz and in using Hooi Loo's services; whether Healthcare is liable for knowing assistance or receipt and for passing off; whether Hooi Loo breached her employment contract; whether a conspiracy was proved; the defences of time bar and acquiescence; and Phang's counterclaim that his employment required reasonable notice.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "Where a company's only two shareholders and directors informally agreed that each could pursue medical-sector business independently, that informal assent (applying Scintronix and Tuitiongenius) released the director from liability for conduct within the agreement. The company's claims also failed because it did not prove the loss or damage, because the six-year limitation period in s 6 of the Limitation Act applied, and because its long knowledge of the business led to acquiescence. Where an employment contract was silent on notice, a director-employee was entitled to reasonable notice, fixed at six months.",
    "significance": "Shows how an informal understanding between shareholder-directors can defeat fiduciary claims, and how the Limitation Act six-year period and acquiescence operate in director conflict disputes, with the court's warnings on pleading and proof of loss.",
