    "subjectArea": "Employment Law / Summary dismissal and gross misconduct; Contract / Implied terms and interpretation; Equity / Account; Debt and set-off",
    "facts": "Mr Matthew Dabbs, a financial advisor who became an Executive Director and Chief Executive Officer of AAM Advisory Pte Ltd, was summarily dismissed on 25 July 2019. An internal investigation had found that he stored illicit files and ran sexually inappropriate searches on his work desktop, sent offensive e-mails to colleagues, and sent client and commission data to his personal Gmail account. He sued for wrongful dismissal and unpaid commissions, and the company counterclaimed for an excess bonus, personal assistant costs and overpaid commissions.",
    "issues": "(a) Whether summary termination under cl 7.3.1 of the Executive Service Agreement was valid, including whether the common law repudiatory-breach principles apply and whether the company's disciplinary guidelines are incorporated; (b) whether the manner of dismissal was wrongful (natural justice; implied term of mutual trust and confidence); (c) whether the claimant is entitled to an account; (d) alleged breaches of cl 9.3 and cl 7A.1; (e) the counterclaims; (f) set-off against the Lapsed Reserve Account.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "Summary dismissal under a clause permitting dismissal for gross default or misconduct, or conduct bringing the employee into serious disrepute, was justified by the employee's storage of illicit files and sexually inappropriate searches on a work desktop, offensive e-mails and breach of confidentiality obligations; the company's disciplinary handbook was not incorporated into the contract and served only as an aid to interpretation. The court was not minded to find an implied term of mutual trust and confidence in Singapore employment contracts, and held that natural justice did not apply to a privately conducted disciplinary hearing. The claimant's claims for wrongful termination and an account failed, while the company recovered its excess bonus and overpaid commissions, net of a set-off of the Lapsed Reserve Account, leaving $85,503.69 payable by the claimant.",
    "significance": "A recent first-instance decision on the test for gross misconduct where the contract is silent, applying RDC Concrete and Phosagro, and on the Singapore position that implied mutual trust and confidence is not established, which is contrary to Mudgal [2026] SGHC 15.",
