    "facts": "Farm to Fork engaged Adamas, whose sole director Mr Kim served as its CFO, under a consultancy agreement requiring three months' prior written notice. On 1 September 2021 Farm to Fork terminated with immediate effect, promising a payment in lieu of $66,660 that it never made. It then sued for breach of confidentiality and non-solicitation obligations, removal obligations and inducement of breach, and Adamas counterclaimed for consultancy fees.",
    "issues": "(a) Whether the immediate termination with unpaid payment in lieu was effective, and whether the agreement remained in force or was accepted as terminated; (b) whether Adamas is entitled to fees to date or only damages; (c) whether Adamas and Mr Kim breached confidentiality, non-solicitation and removal obligations, and whether the equitable breach of confidence and inducement claims succeed; (d) remedies, including delivery up, injunctions and equitable damages.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "The termination with payment in lieu was not effective because no payment was made, and the court would reject an implied term allowing termination by merely promising to pay. However, under Malaysian law (following Francis and Fung Keong rather than Dato' Abdullah), the client's wrongful repudiation terminated the personal-services agreement, so specific performance was unavailable and the consultant's remedy was damages equal to three months' fees ($66,660). Mr Kim's disclosures to investors and shareholders of a reasonably suspected accounting misrepresentation were required by law or justified in the public interest, so the confidentiality and non-solicitation claims failed.",
    "significance": "It shows that a client who purports to terminate on payment in lieu must actually pay, and that a wrongful termination of a personal-services contract limits the consultant to notice-period damages. It also applies the public-interest defence to confidentiality claims where there is a reasonable suspicion of accounting misrepresentation.",
