    "facts": "Grover, an NDF broker, signed an employment agreement with GFI in November 2017, together with two loan agreements totalling about S$2.55m for a signing-on bonus and a settlement with his former employer. After his employment was novated to BGC, BGC terminated it on 22 September 2021 for failing the 2.5:1 Performance Ratio. The plaintiffs sued for repayment of US$1,879,981.45 (to GFI) and US$158,765.97 (to BGC), and Grover counterclaimed for wrongful termination damages and unpaid bonuses.",
    "issues": "(a) Whether an alleged oral employment agreement was binding, and whether the written agreement with the Performance Ratio was binding on Grover (non est factum; entire agreement clause); (b) whether BGC lawfully terminated for failing the Performance Ratio, and whether any implied duty of mutual trust and confidence was breached; (c) whether the loans became repayable when Grover ceased to be a partner; (d) whether Grover was entitled to unpaid bonuses and whether BGC lawfully withheld them.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "The non est factum defence failed: the oral in-principle terms and the written Performance Ratio clause were not radically different, Grover was careless in signing a 13-page contract he had days to read, and his conduct showed he knew he was bound. The entire agreement clause superseded the alleged oral terms, and BGC's express right to terminate on notice under cl 4.2.2 was not subject to a reasonableness limit, following Dong Wei. The loans fell due when Grover ceased to be a partner on termination, and the bonus clause was construed as discretionary, so withholding the bonuses was reasonable.",
    "significance": "It confirms how narrow the non est factum doctrine is for employees who sign contracts without reading them, and that an express performance-based termination right will be enforced without an implied reasonableness limit.",
    "statutesCited": [],
    "unreadable": false,
    "notes": "The caption reads 'BGC Partners (Singapore) Ltd'; the parties line gives 'Limited'. The BGC Holdings Partnership Agreement is governed by Delaware law, proved by expert evidence, but no statute section is cited. The court left open whether Singapore employment contracts imply a term of mutual trust and confidence. The judge reserved further interest under cl 3 of the Loan Agreements and costs, including the indemnity costs claim. The loans were disbursed in S$, but the claims are framed in US$ and were not challenged on that basis."
