    "facts": "Carlsberg South Asia Pte Ltd (CSAPL) employed Pawan Kumar Jagetia as its Senior Vice President from April 2018 until his dismissal in June 2019. CSAPL claimed S$232,000 back from him on the footing that he had impliedly agreed to relocate to Singapore with his family, and he counterclaimed for unpaid bonuses, a salary increment, the Repatriation Allowance and salary in lieu of notice.",
    "issues": "(1) Whether a term requiring Mr Jagetia to relocate to Singapore with his family could be implied; (2) whether CSAPL could recover the Annual Benefits Package and Relocation Allowance paid; (3) the quantum of the 2018 STI and the scope of CSAPL's discretion over it; (4) whether CSAPL had to pay his CIPL STI for January to March 2018; (5) whether CSAPL had to give an annual salary increment under cl 6.2; (6) whether CSAPL could terminate without notice under cl 11.5, or owed three months' salary in lieu under cl 11.4.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "No relocation term could be implied: the parties had contemplated relocation, and cl 1.4 ('accompanying family, if any') shows they chose not to impose it, so the gap was not a true gap the court could fill. CSAPL could not dismiss Mr Jagetia without notice under cl 11.5 because the breaches it relied on did not amount to repudiatory breach, so he was entitled to three months' salary in lieu under cl 11.4. CSAPL was also liable for his CIPL STI for January to March 2018, because cll 1.2, 1.3 and 7.1 read together placed that payment on CSAPL.",
    "significance": "It applies the three-step Sembcorp test strictly, refusing to imply a term where the parties considered the point but did not record it, and it reads an 'any breach' termination right as limited to repudiatory breach.",
