    "facts": "The employer dismissed Long Kim Wing, a former director and employee, without notice on 14 June 2012 for alleged misconduct, namely creating or being involved in a forged offer letter for another employee and authorising an unapproved S$30,000 advance payment to that employee. He sued for one month's salary in lieu of notice, severance pay, two months' salary under the employer's general service terms, director's fees, expenses and damages for loss of future employment.",
    "issues": "(1) Whether the employer proved misconduct justifying dismissal without notice; (2) whether the employer conducted the 'due inquiry' required by cl 5(1) of its General Service Terms; (3) the damages payable for any failure to hold a due inquiry; (4) the claims for director's fees, expenses and damages for loss of similar employment; (5) the employer's counterclaim for the S$30,000 advance.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "The employer proved misconduct: Mr Long created or was involved in creating the forged offer letter, or at least tried to use it to deceive, and he authorised the S$30,000 advance without approval, so the claims for notice pay and severance failed. However, 'due inquiry' requires more than making inquiries, since the employee must be told the allegations and the evidence and given a real chance to respond, and the employer had not done this, so he recovered seven days' salary (S$5,512.98) for the time a proper inquiry would have taken.",
