    "facts": "Hengxin Technology Ltd, a Singapore-listed cable manufacturer, removed its executive directors Jiang Wei and Qian Lirong in January 2007, and the handover at its Jiangsu subsidiary then failed. The company terminated Qian's service agreement by letter on 15 February 2007 and accepted Jiang's resignation in April 2007, each with payment in lieu of six months' notice. It then sued both men for breach of fiduciary duties, confidentiality and non-competition, and they counterclaimed for unpaid bonuses.",
    "issues": "(1) Which party was in repudiatory breach of the service agreements; (2) whether the non-competition clause (cl 8 and its Schedule) and the confidentiality clause (cl 3) were enforceable and breached; (3) whether the defendants breached their fiduciary duties; (4) whether the defendants were entitled to their prorated bonuses under cl 4.2; (5) whether the company could rely on misconduct raised only after termination; (6) the withholding tax claim against Qian.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "The non-competition clause was void because cl 2(a) had no territorial limit and 'business' was undefined, and the company showed no legitimate proprietary interest, since the alleged confidential pricing and R&D information was not proved and the defendants' knowledge was general industry experience. Terminating without cause and without the six months' notice required by cl 2.2 was a repudiatory breach, so the defendants were entitled to their accrued prorated bonuses under cl 4.2. The company could not defeat those bonuses by relying on misconduct it raised only after termination.",
