    "issues": "(1) Whether clause 3.1(i) allowed Amara to end the two managers' services early; (2) the quantum of SIH's recoverable perquisite and tax claims, including the release in clause 1.2; (3) whether Schedule G imposed an unenforceable penalty; (4) the rate of interest on the NOK sum; (5) Amara's counterclaim for sums said to be owed by SIH.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "Read with the rest of the Exit Agreement, clause 3.1(i) fixed the managers' services at three months that neither party could unilaterally shorten, so Amara's termination on 15 June 1986 was wrongful. SIH could recover only the perquisites it actually expended or was liable to expend in that period, and claims released by clause 1.2 were barred. Schedule G was not a penalty because it imposed no obligation whose breach triggered the larger sum; it fixed the terms on which a smaller payment would settle the claim, so the balance of NOK 803,588.39 was due.",
    "significance": "It shows a court reading a single clause against the whole agreement, limiting damages for wrongful termination to the innocent party's actual or contractually due outlay so that it does not profit from the breach, and treating a pay-by-deadline settlement term as outside the penalty rule.",
