    "facts": "The plaintiff, a chief executive officer hired by the defendant, left employment on 31 October 2011. He sought a declaration that the Appendix B payments (cash equivalents of forfeited Ingram Micro retention benefits, falling due between March 2012 and April 2013) were payable under the 'Accrued Obligations' clause after termination for any reason.",
    "issues": "Whether the Appendix B payments falling due after termination were payable under cl XVIII(5), including the First Limb (payment at the times they would have been provided had employment not terminated) and the 'accrued but unpaid benefits' wording in the definition at subpart (iii); the effect of the undefined 'schedule' and arithmetic error in Addendum II; and the timing of payment.",
    "ratioDecidendi": "The First Limb of cl XVIII(5) operated as a specific modification of the general definition of 'Accrued Obligations' for the Appendix B payments, so they remained payable on their scheduled dates despite termination. The court read 'accrued but unpaid' in subpart (iii) accordingly, because the defendant's narrower reading would leave the First Limb with no effect. The plaintiff was held entitled to the six sums totalling US$4,561,450 on the dates in Appendix B, with interest on late payment and costs.",
    "significance": "Shows how a court resolves conflicting clauses in a badly drafted contract by reading the agreement as a whole, giving effect to every provision, and preferring a specific clause over a general definition.",
